# Tree Comparison - Symmetric Tree, Same Tree and Same Structure

## Main Idea

While working with tree comparison problems, I noticed that several problems use almost the same recursive pattern.

The main difference is not the recursion itself, but:

- What should be compared?
- Should the branches be compared directly or as mirrors?
- Do the values matter?

This led to three closely related ideas:

    Same Tree
        -> left with left, right with right, values matter

    Same Structure
        -> left with left, right with right, values do not matter

    Symmetric Tree
        -> left with right, right with left, values matter

---

# 101. Symmetric Tree

[LeetCode - Symmetric Tree](https://leetcode.com/problems/symmetric-tree/)

## My Approach

At first, I thought about using level order traversal with a queue, since symmetry seems related to comparing nodes level by level.

But while thinking about how to actually compare the nodes, the queue approach started getting messy.

Then I noticed something simpler:

The root divides the tree into two separate subtrees.

So instead of asking:

"Is this whole tree symmetric?"

I can ask:

"Are the left and right subtrees mirrors of each other?"

That makes the problem much more natural for recursion.

---

## The Mirror Observation

For two subtrees to be mirrors:

- The left side of the first tree must match the right side of the second tree.
- The right side of the first tree must match the left side of the second tree.

So:

    root2.left  <-> root3.right
    root2.right <-> root3.left

Every recursive call therefore compares opposite branches.

---

## The Three Cases

When comparing two nodes, there are three important cases.

### 1. Both are null

    root2 = null
    root3 = null

Both sides ended at the same time.

Therefore, this part is symmetric.

### 2. Only one is null

    root2 = null
    root3 = node

or the opposite.

One tree has a node where the other does not.

Therefore, their structures are different.

### 3. Values are different

If both nodes exist but their values are different, they cannot be mirrors.

If none of these conditions fail, I recursively compare the opposite branches.

---

## Main Insight

The interesting part of this problem was not learning a new traversal.

It was changing the way I looked at the tree.

Initially:

    Symmetry
        |
        v
    Level Order
        |
        v
      Queue

But that started getting complicated.

Then I noticed:

    Symmetry
        |
        v
    Two Subtrees
        |
        v
    Compare Them as Mirrors
        |
        v
    Recursively Compare Opposite Branches

Once I saw the tree as two halves, the recursive solution became almost natural.

---

# Same Tree

After understanding the mirror comparison, comparing two trees directly becomes even simpler.

For two trees to be completely the same:

- Both nodes must be null at the same time.
- One cannot be null while the other exists.
- Their values must be equal.
- Their left subtrees must be the same.
- Their right subtrees must be the same.

The important difference from Symmetric Tree is the direction of comparison.

Instead of:

    left  <-> right
    right <-> left

we simply compare:

    left  <-> left
    right <-> right

So the recursion asks whether corresponding positions in both trees are the same.

---

# Same Structure

Now we can remove one condition.

Suppose we don't care about the values at all.

We only want to know:

"Do these two trees have the same shape?"

The recursive structure remains exactly the same:

    left  <-> left
    right <-> right

The only difference is that the value comparison disappears.

So:

    Same Tree
    = Same Structure + Same Values

while:

    Same Structure
    = Same Tree Comparison - Value Constraint

---

# The Bigger Pattern

This helped me recognize a more general pattern in tree recursion.

When comparing two trees, first decide:

"What exactly am I trying to compare?"

### Same Tree

    Structure + Values

    left  <-> left
    right <-> right

### Same Structure

    Structure only

    left  <-> left
    right <-> right

### Symmetric Tree

    Mirror Structure + Values

    left  <-> right
    right <-> left

The recursive skeleton stays almost identical.

What changes is the comparison rule.

---

## The Important Connection

These are not really three completely different recursive ideas.

They are variations of the same basic tree-comparison pattern.

The difficult part is often not writing the recursion.

The difficult part is identifying:

    What should be compared?

    Left with left?
    Left with right?

    Do values matter?

Once those conditions are clear, the recursive solution follows naturally.

---

## Complexity

For all three approaches:

Time Complexity:

    O(n)

Every relevant node is visited once.

Space Complexity:

    O(h)

where h is the height of the tree because of the recursion stack.

For a balanced tree:

    O(log n)

For a completely skewed tree:

    O(n)

---

## What I Learned

The biggest takeaway from these problems is that a recursive problem often becomes simple once I identify exactly what smaller version of the same question I need to ask.

For Symmetric Tree, the smaller question was not:

"Is this subtree symmetric?"

It was:

"Are these two subtrees mirrors of each other?"

For Same Tree, the question becomes:

"Are these two corresponding subtrees the same?"

And for Same Structure:

"Do these two corresponding subtrees have the same shape?"

So the recursion itself is almost the same.

The real insight is deciding the comparison relationship before writing the recursion.